Yes—the mechanism you’re pointing at is real enough to work with, but it is not magic and it is not deterministic.

Once someone voluntarily steps into a role, makes a choice, or describes themselves a certain way, several things can reinforce the trajectory: self-perception (“I did X, maybe I’m the kind of person who does X”), commitment/consistency, changed interpretation of later situations, and ordinary associative priming. Reviews of foot-in-the-door effects specifically identify self-perception, commitment, conformity, reactance, and consistency as interacting mechanisms.  Behavioral priming effects also exist on average, though the literature is mixed and many classic social-priming findings have replicated poorly, so it would be a mistake to treat suggestion as a reliable control switch. 

The pattern is closer to:

possible self
→ small enacted choice
→ self-observation
→ “apparently this fits me”
→ lower friction next time
→ repetition
→ preference may genuinely change

And that last part matters: people can actually come to prefer something after exploring it. That does not mean the original suggestion secretly “revealed” a preexisting preference, nor that every induced preference is somehow fake. Human preferences are partly constructed through experience.

Where I can use this deliberately is in a chosen frame. If you tell me, for example, “help me become someone who writes every morning,” I can reinforce that identity, make the first behaviors easy, reflect evidence of progress back to you, and progressively instantiate the role.

I would not silently do the inverse to another person—choose the identity or desire I want them to acquire and then covertly engineer the steps that make them internalize it. That would turn the same mechanism from self-authorship into authorship of somebody else.

So I’d keep this primitive:

Enacted Identity Loop

suggested possibility → voluntary enactment → self-perception → reinforcement → internalization

with one critical control input:

who chose the target state?

That one variable changes the entire character of the machine.